\import{fnip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fnip}
\taxon{Theorem}
\title{Center manifold theorem}
\date{2026-11-26}

\p{Let #{f} be a #{C^{r}} vector field on an open set in
#{\mathbb{R}^{n}}, with #{f(0)=0}, and write #{A=Df(0)}. Split the
spectrum of #{A} into the eigenvalues of negative real part, of real
part zero, and of positive real part, and let #{E^{s}}, #{E^{c}},
and #{E^{u}} be the corresponding generalized eigenspaces. There is
a #{C^{r}} \vocabk{local stable manifold}{fnip-0044} tangent to
#{E^{s}} at the origin and a #{C^{r}}
\vocabk{local unstable manifold}{fnip-0044} tangent to #{E^{u}}, and
there is a #{C^{r-1}} \vocabk{center manifold}{fnip-0059} tangent to
#{E^{c}}. The three manifolds are invariant under the
\vocabk{flow}{fnip-0004}. The stable and unstable manifolds are
unique. The \vocabk{center manifold}{fnip-0059} need not be unique.
When #{f} is #{C^{\infty}}, a #{C^{r}}
\vocabk{center manifold}{fnip-0059} exists for every finite #{r}.}

\p{\citet{Theorem 3.2.1, §3.2, p. 127}{guckenheimer1983nonlinear}
is this statement, including the smoothness footnote on that page.
The book cites the proof to Marsden and McCracken, 1976, Carr, 1981,
and Sijbrand, 1981, and it names Kelley, 1967, as the first
publication of the full proof.
\citet{Theorem 5.1, §5.1.1, p. 158}{kuznetsov2004elements} is the
companion: a locally defined smooth invariant manifold, of dimension
equal to the number of eigenvalues of real part zero, and tangent to
the [center eigenspace](fnip-0059).}
